This capability uses cryptographic services to verify the source, authenticity, and integrity of digital health records and transactions. It often involves the use of digital certificates and signatures.&#xD;
&#xD;
The purpose is to establish technical trust in the &#xD;
absence of a central authority for every transaction. It ensures that any recipient can prove a record is authentic and has not been tampered with.&#xD;
&#xD;
Example: A verification service that checks the digital signature on an e-prescription before it is filled by a pharmacy.&#xD;
